{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>If a woman makes a vow to YHWH.</b> A woman from twelve years and upward is called an adult, and her vows and her oaths are binding. From eleven years and upward, when she is a minor, they examine her throughout that year: if she knows for whose name she vowed and for whose name she consecrated, her words are binding, like the law of a minor male from twelve years and upward; but before that, their words are nothing. And after twelve years for a woman and after thirteen years for a man, even though they do not know for whose name they vowed and consecrated, their words are binding. This perashah will explain for us, in matters of vows, many great matters in few words. And the laws of the woman fall into four categories, and they are: na'arah, betrothed (arusah), married (nesu'ah), and widow. Na'arah is from the beginning of twelve years until the end of six months — if she has a father, she is in his domain, and he may annul all her vows and her oaths on the day he hears them, for the presumption is that she vowed on the condition that her father would want it, as if she stipulated explicitly — unlike a son; and similarly with the sale of a daughter, that a father is permitted to sell his daughter as a maidservant, but not so a son. From these six months onward she is called bogeret (of age) and she is in her own domain; and this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: once she has reached majority, her father no longer has authority over her, and all her vows and her oaths are like the vow of a widow and a divorcée, of which it is written: ״כל אשר אסרה על נפשה יקום עליה״. If she vowed while still in her father's domain and he annulled it on the day he heard, everything is void. And what is said, ״וה' יסלח לה״ — the Rabbis, of blessed memory, raised the difficulty: about what is the verse speaking? About a matter that requires forgiveness, such as he annulled for her and she did not know and transgressed her vow according to her assumption; and certainly one who thinks he is committing a transgression, even though he is not, requires forgiveness. Betrothed (arusah) is what is written: ״ואם היו תהיה לאיש ונדריה עליה״ — this הויה is the language of betrothal (erusin), and this speaks of a na'arah who is betrothed, who cannot have annulment through the father alone or through the betrothed alone, but only through both of them. And thus we learned: a na'arah who is betrothed — her father and her husband annul her vows. And it is as if she vowed explicitly on the condition of both of them. And it is required that both hear on the same day and annul on that day. If the betrothed dies, she reverts to her father's domain, and her father annuls for her as if she had vowed before she was betrothed. And if the father dies after she was betrothed and she vowed after his death, the betrothed does not annul the vows of his betrothed until she enters the wedding canopy."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:07.218955309Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}